  "question": {
    "html": "<p>Kepler’s Third law relates the orbital period <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"T\"></span> of a planet about its sun to its orbital radius <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"r\"></span>. The mass of the Sun is <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"M\"></span>.</p>\n<p>What is a correct algebraic form of the law?</p>\n<ol class=\"mcq-options\"><li data-label=\"A\"><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"T = \\frac{2 π {r}^{1.5}}{{( G M )}^{0.5}}\"></span></li><li data-label=\"B\"><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"T = \\frac{2 π {r}^{1.5}}{G M}\"></span></li><li data-label=\"C\"><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"T = \\frac{4 π {r}^{0.67}}{{( G M )}^{2}}\"></span></li><li data-label=\"D\"><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"T = \\frac{4 π {r}^{0.67}}{G M}\"></span></li></ol>"
  },
